  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =16x-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{1}{4}) & =16-\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}}=-112<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(1) & =16-\\frac{2}{(1)^{3}}=14>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is indeed a point of inflection.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
